    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Using a proof by contradiction, show that $\\log_6 3$ and $\\log_6 2$ are both irrational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p8-q10-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Both numbers are irrational.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p8-q10-a-short-reason",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "A rational value would give ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "6^m=3^n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", contradicting parity, or ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "6^m=2^n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", contradicting divisibility by 3, for positive integers m and n.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Suppose $\\log_6 3$ is rational. Since it is positive, write $\\log_6 3=\\frac{m}{n}$ for positive integers $m,n$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_6 3&=\\frac{m}{n}\\\\6^{m/n}&=3\\\\(6^{m/n})^n&=3^n\\\\6^m&=3^n\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side is even and the right-hand side is odd, a contradiction. Therefore $\\log_6 3$ is irrational.\n\nSuppose $\\log_6 2$ is rational. Since it is positive, write $\\log_6 2=\\frac{r}{s}$ for positive integers $r,s$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_6 2&=\\frac{r}{s}\\\\6^{r/s}&=2\\\\(6^{r/s})^s&=2^s\\\\6^r&=2^s\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side is divisible by 3, whereas the right-hand side is not, a contradiction. Therefore $\\log_6 2$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that the sum of two irrational numbers may be rational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\log_6 3+\\log_6 2=1$, which is rational.",
          "worked": "Both $\\log_6 3$ and $\\log_6 2$ are irrational by part (a), but\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_6 3+\\log_6 2&=\\log_6(3\\times2)\\\\\\log_6 3+\\log_6 2&=\\log_6 6\\\\\\log_6 3+\\log_6 2&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nSince 1 is rational, the sum of two irrational numbers may be rational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
